    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Electromagnetic waves",
     "md": "All electromagnetic waves are **transverse**, travel at **3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum** and carry energy. Uses: **radio** (broadcasting), **microwaves** (cooking, mobile phones), **infrared** (remote controls, heaters), **light** (sight), **ultraviolet** (sterilising), **X-rays** (medical images), **gamma rays** (treating cancer). Too much UV, X-ray or gamma radiation is harmful to living cells."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Do not confuse amplitude (height from the middle) with the crest-to-trough height, which is twice as large.\n- Frequency is waves per second, not seconds per wave (that is the period).\n- Sound is longitudinal and needs a medium; electromagnetic waves do not."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Wave equation",
     "statement": "A sound wave in air has a frequency of 50 Hz and speed 340 m/s. Find its wavelength.",
     "steps": [
      "λ = v ÷ f.",
      "340 ÷ 50 = 6.8 m."
     ],
     "answer": "6.8 m"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Radio wave",
     "statement": "A radio station broadcasts at 100 MHz (1 × 10⁸ Hz). Find the wavelength (v = 3 × 10⁸ m/s).",
     "steps": [
      "λ = v ÷ f = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 1 × 10⁸.",
      "= 3 m."
     ],
     "answer": "3 m"
    },
    {
     "type": "exercise",
     "ref": "form4-physics-waves-e01"
