     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Pitch, loudness and quality",
     "md": "**Pitch** depends on **frequency**: higher frequency means higher pitch. **Loudness** depends on **amplitude**: bigger amplitude means louder. **Quality (timbre)** is the character of the sound that lets us tell a guitar from a drum even at the same pitch and loudness. Very loud noise can damage hearing."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Echoes and speed",
     "md": "An **echo** is a reflection of sound. If the sound travels to a wall and back in time t: **distance to the wall = v × t ÷ 2**. Thunder: the light arrives almost instantly, so **distance = 340 × time between flash and thunder**. **Sonar** uses ultrasound echoes to find the depth of the sea. Medical **ultrasound** scans use high-frequency sound."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Divide by 2 for echoes: the sound travels there **and back**.\n- Sound is not faster in air than in solids; it is slower.\n- Loudness is not the same as pitch."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Echo",
     "statement": "A boy claps and hears an echo from a cliff 0.40 s later. How far away is the cliff? (v = 340 m/s)",
     "steps": [
      "Total distance = 340 × 0.40 = 136 m (there and back).",
      "Distance to the cliff = 136 ÷ 2 = 68 m."
     ],
     "answer": "68 m"
    },
